# Chapter 2 · Nutrition (owner, 12 Nov 2026: levels and clean wording as in the Obstetrics book).
# Card: (old index, section, question, answer, level). Facts, numbers and doses are unchanged; shorthand is written out in full.
# Dropped: feeding 10 (the same card is micronutrients 36); micronutrients 29 (same as feeding 13); micronutrients 30 (same as feeding 34).
CARDS = {
'feeding': [
(0, 'Breastfeeding', 'When should breastfeeding start after birth?', 'Within the first hour of birth.', 1),
(1, 'Breastfeeding', 'For how long is exclusive breastfeeding recommended?', 'For 6 months. Breastfeeding then continues to 2 years of age or beyond.', 1),
(2, 'Breastfeeding', 'Which hormone produces breast milk?', 'Prolactin, from the anterior pituitary. It is stimulated by suckling.', 1),
(3, 'Breastfeeding', 'Which hormone causes milk ejection (the let-down reflex)?', 'Oxytocin, from the posterior pituitary. It is inhibited by stress and pain.', 1),
(4, 'Breastfeeding', 'How does colostrum differ from mature milk?', 'Colostrum has more protein, IgA, white cells and vitamin A, and less fat and lactose. It is also a mild laxative.', 2),
(5, 'Breastfeeding', 'Which part of a feed is richer in fat?', 'The hindmilk (the end of the feed), which has about 3 times the fat of the foremilk.', 1),
(6, 'Breastfeeding', 'How does the protein of breast milk compare with that of cow\'s milk?', 'Breast milk has less protein (about one third), and it is mainly whey.', 2),
(7, 'Breastfeeding', 'What is the calcium-to-phosphorus ratio of breast milk?', '2 : 1, which gives better calcium absorption.', 2),
(8, 'Breastfeeding', 'Why is the iron in breast milk sufficient even though the amount is low?', 'About 50% of it is absorbed (high bioavailability).', 2),
(9, 'Breastfeeding', 'Which vitamin is not adequately present in breast milk?', 'Vitamin K. Vitamin D is also low.', 1),
(11, 'Breastfeeding', 'Name four anti-infective factors in breast milk.', 'Secretory IgA, lactoferrin, lysozyme and the bifidus factor; breast milk also contains white cells.', 1),
(12, 'Breastfeeding', 'How does lactoferrin protect the infant?', 'It binds iron and deprives bacteria such as E. coli of it, so it is bacteriostatic.', 2),
(13, 'Breastfeeding', 'A breastfed infant has a vegan mother. Which deficiency is likely?', 'Vitamin B12 deficiency, causing megaloblastic anaemia.', 2),
(14, 'Practice', 'What are the signs of good attachment at the breast?', 'The chin touches the breast, the mouth is wide open, the lower lip is turned out, and more areola is seen above the mouth than below it.', 1),
(15, 'Practice', 'What is the minimum number of breastfeeds in 24 hours?', 'At least 8, on demand, by day and by night.', 1),
(16, 'Practice', 'What is the best sign that a breastfed baby is getting enough milk?', 'Adequate weight gain (20–30 g per day).', 2),
(17, 'Practice', 'How many wet nappies a day show adequate milk intake?', '6 or more wet nappies a day.', 1),
(18, 'Practice', 'What is the first step for a breastfed baby with poor weight gain?', 'Observe a full feed and correct the position and attachment.', 2),
(19, 'Problems', 'What is the commonest cause of sore, cracked nipples?', 'Poor attachment at the breast. Candida infection is another cause.', 1),
(20, 'Problems', 'How is mastitis treated?', 'Breastfeeding continues, with flucloxacillin (cloxacillin) and analgesia.', 2),
(21, 'Problems', 'How is a breast abscess treated?', 'Drainage and antibiotics; the baby feeds from the other breast.', 2),
(22, 'Contraindications', 'What is the classic absolute contraindication to breastfeeding in the infant?', 'Galactosaemia.', 1),
(23, 'Contraindications', 'Which maternal drugs prevent breastfeeding?', 'Anticancer chemotherapy and radioactive drugs.', 2),
(24, 'Contraindications', 'Can a mother with hepatitis B breastfeed?', 'Yes, after the baby has received hepatitis B immunoglobulin and hepatitis B vaccine.', 2),
(25, 'Contraindications', 'Can a mother with tuberculosis on treatment breastfeed?', 'Yes. The baby receives isoniazid prophylaxis.', 2),
(26, 'Contraindications', 'How should an HIV-positive mother on antiretroviral therapy feed her baby (WHO)?', 'She breastfeeds exclusively for 6 months, then continues breastfeeding with complementary food.', 3),
(27, 'Formula', 'What daily volume of formula does an infant need?', 'About 150 ml/kg/day, given in 6–8 feeds.', 1),
(28, 'Formula', 'What is the result of over-diluted formula?', 'Poor weight gain and failure to thrive.', 2),
(29, 'Formula', 'What is the result of over-concentrated formula?', 'Hypernatraemic dehydration and obesity.', 2),
(30, 'Formula', 'Which formula is used for lactose intolerance or galactosaemia?', 'A lactose-free (soy-based) formula.', 2),
(31, 'Formula', 'Which formula is used for cow\'s milk protein allergy?', 'An extensively hydrolysed formula, or an amino acid formula. Soy formula is not used.', 3),
(32, 'Formula', 'Which formula is used for phenylketonuria?', 'A low-phenylalanine formula.', 1),
(33, 'Formula', 'What is the problem with whole cow\'s milk before 1 year of age?', 'Iron deficiency anaemia.', 2),
(34, 'Formula', 'What are the problems with goat\'s milk?', 'Folate and vitamin B12 deficiency (megaloblastic anaemia), and brucellosis if the milk is not boiled.', 2),
(35, 'Weaning', 'When does complementary feeding start?', 'At 6 months of age.', 1),
(36, 'Weaning', 'Why should complementary feeding not start before 4 months?', 'The gut is immature, infections and allergy increase, and the baby takes less breast milk.', 2),
(37, 'Weaning', 'How many meals a day does an infant of 6–8 months need?', '2–3 meals and 1–2 snacks a day, with breastfeeding.', 1),
(38, 'Weaning', 'How many meals a day does a child of 9–24 months need?', '3–4 meals and 1–2 snacks a day, with breastfeeding.', 1),
(39, 'Weaning', 'Which food must not be given under 1 year of age because of botulism?', 'Honey.', 1),
(40, 'Weaning', 'What are the results of late weaning?', 'Severe acute malnutrition without oedema (marasmus), iron deficiency anaemia and rickets.', 2),
(41, 'Weaning', 'What is the IMCI feeding advice during and after an illness?', 'Continue feeding, and give one extra meal a day for 2 weeks.', 2),
],
'sam': [
(0, 'Definition', 'What are the three criteria of severe acute malnutrition (SAM) in children aged 6–59 months? (Any one is enough.)', 'A mid-upper arm circumference (MUAC) below 11.5 cm, a weight-for-height below −3 standard deviations, or bilateral pitting oedema.', 1),
(1, 'Definition', 'What is the MUAC range of moderate acute malnutrition (MAM)?', 'From 11.5 cm to below 12.5 cm; the weight-for-height is between −3 and −2 standard deviations.', 1),
(2, 'Definition', 'What are the two clinical forms of SAM?', 'SAM without oedema (marasmus) and SAM with oedema (kwashiorkor). Oedema together with severe wasting is the most severe form.', 1),
(3, 'Definition', 'A child has a low weight-for-age but a normal weight-for-height. Is the child wasted?', 'No. This is underweight, not wasting; wasting is a low weight-for-height.', 3),
(4, 'Definition', 'A child has bilateral pitting oedema and a normal MUAC. What is the diagnosis?', 'SAM with oedema (oedematous malnutrition), whatever the weight or MUAC.', 3),
(5, 'SAM without oedema', 'What is the dietary cause of SAM without oedema?', 'A deficiency of total energy (calories and protein): starvation.', 1),
(6, 'SAM without oedema', 'At what age does SAM without oedema usually occur?', 'In infancy (under 1 year of age).', 1),
(7, 'SAM without oedema', 'What are the behaviour and appetite of a child with SAM without oedema?', 'The child is alert, irritable and hungry.', 2),
(8, 'SAM without oedema', 'Which is the last site to lose fat in SAM without oedema?', 'The buccal pad of fat in the cheeks; its loss gives the "old man" face.', 2),
(9, 'SAM with oedema', 'What is the dietary cause of SAM with oedema?', 'Protein deficiency with fairly adequate calories (a starchy diet), often triggered by infection.', 2),
(10, 'SAM with oedema', 'At what age does SAM with oedema usually occur?', 'At 1–3 years, after weaning.', 1),
(11, 'SAM with oedema', 'Which feature is essential for the diagnosis of SAM with oedema (kwashiorkor)?', 'Bilateral pitting oedema.', 1),
(12, 'SAM with oedema', 'What are the constant features of SAM with oedema?', 'Oedema, growth failure, mental changes (apathy), and muscle wasting with preserved fat.', 2),
(13, 'SAM with oedema', 'What is the flag sign?', 'Alternating pale and dark bands along the hair, reflecting periods of poor and better nutrition.', 1),
(14, 'SAM with oedema', 'What is flaky-paint dermatosis?', 'Dark patches of skin that peel off and leave raw skin, on the legs, buttocks and napkin area.', 1),
(15, 'SAM with oedema', 'What causes the hepatomegaly of SAM with oedema?', 'Fatty infiltration of the liver: apolipoprotein is low, so fat cannot leave the liver.', 3),
(16, 'SAM with oedema', 'What is the most characteristic laboratory finding in SAM with oedema?', 'A low serum albumin.', 2),
(17, 'SAM with oedema', 'Which has the worse prognosis: SAM without oedema or SAM with oedema?', 'SAM with oedema, because infections and electrolyte problems are more common.', 2),
(18, 'SAM with oedema', 'Which grade of oedema reaches the face, and what is done?', 'Grade +++ (severe, generalised oedema). The child is admitted.', 2),
(19, 'Complications', 'What is the commonest cause of death in SAM?', 'Infection: bronchopneumonia and sepsis.', 1),
(20, 'Complications', 'How is hypoglycaemia defined in SAM?', 'A blood glucose below 3 mmol/L (54 mg/dL).', 1),
(21, 'Complications', 'How is hypothermia defined in SAM?', 'An axillary temperature below 35 °C (rectal temperature below 35.5 °C).', 1),
(22, 'Complications', 'What are the reliable signs of dehydration in SAM?', 'A history of watery diarrhoea, recently sunken eyes, a weak radial pulse and little urine.', 3),
(23, 'Complications', 'What is refeeding syndrome?', 'Feeding a starved child too fast causes low phosphate, potassium and magnesium, which can lead to heart failure.', 3),
(24, 'Where to treat', 'What are the indications to admit a child with SAM?', 'A medical complication, a failed appetite test, oedema grade +++, oedema with severe wasting, or an infant under 6 months who is not feeding well.', 2),
(25, 'Where to treat', 'How is the appetite test done?', 'The child is offered the test amount of ready-to-use therapeutic food (RUTF) for his weight. If he eats it within 30 minutes he passes and is treated in the outpatient therapeutic programme (OTP).', 2),
(26, 'Stabilisation', 'How is hypoglycaemia treated in a conscious child with SAM?', '50 ml of 10% glucose (or sugar water) by mouth or nasogastric tube, followed by F-75.', 2),
(27, 'Stabilisation', 'How is hypoglycaemia treated in an unconscious child with SAM?', '10% glucose 5 ml/kg intravenously, then 50 ml by nasogastric tube.', 2),
(28, 'Stabilisation', 'How is a child with SAM rewarmed?', 'Skin-to-skin (kangaroo) contact with the mother, covering the head, a warm room and immediate feeding. Hot-water bottles are not used.', 2),
(29, 'Stabilisation', 'Which fluid is used to rehydrate a child with SAM who is not in shock, and how?', 'ReSoMal: 5 ml/kg every 30 minutes for 2 hours, then 5–10 ml/kg per hour for 4–10 hours.', 2),
(30, 'Stabilisation', 'What are the sodium and potassium contents of ReSoMal?', 'Sodium 45 and potassium 40 mmol/L (standard ORS: sodium 75 and potassium 20 mmol/L).', 2),
(31, 'Stabilisation', 'What is the only indication for intravenous fluid in SAM?', 'Shock: 15 ml/kg over 1 hour.', 2),
(32, 'Stabilisation', 'How is severe anaemia (haemoglobin below 4 g/dL) treated in SAM?', 'Packed cells or whole blood 10 ml/kg over 3 hours.', 2),
(33, 'Stabilisation', 'Are diuretics used for the oedema of SAM?', 'No, never.', 2),
(34, 'Infection', 'Which antibiotic is given in SAM without complications?', 'Amoxicillin 15 mg/kg orally 8-hourly for 5 days.', 2),
(35, 'Infection', 'Which antibiotics are given in complicated SAM?', 'Ampicillin 50 mg/kg 6-hourly for 2 days, then amoxicillin, together with gentamicin 7.5 mg/kg daily for 7 days.', 2),
(36, 'Micronutrients', 'When is iron started in SAM?', 'In the rehabilitation phase (week 2), when the child is gaining weight.', 3),
(37, 'Micronutrients', 'When is high-dose vitamin A given in SAM, and what is the dose over 12 months of age?', 'Only with eye signs or measles, because the therapeutic feeds already contain vitamin A: 200 000 IU on days 1, 2 and 14.', 3),
(38, 'Feeding', 'What are the energy and protein contents of F-75?', '75 kcal and 0.9 g of protein per 100 ml.', 1),
(39, 'Feeding', 'How is F-75 given on days 1–2?', '11 ml/kg every 2 hours (12 feeds a day), which is 130 ml/kg/day.', 2),
(40, 'Feeding', 'When is F-75 changed to F-100 or RUTF?', 'When the appetite returns and the oedema is reducing.', 2),
(41, 'Feeding', 'What is the target weight gain in the rehabilitation phase?', 'More than 10 g/kg/day.', 1),
(42, 'Feeding', 'During transition to F-100, the pulse rises by 25/min and the breathing by 5/min. What should be done?', 'These are warning signs of heart failure: the feed volume is reduced.', 3),
(43, 'Outpatient', 'What is the energy content of one sachet of RUTF?', '500 kcal in 92 g.', 1),
(44, 'Outpatient', 'What is the main advantage of RUTF at home?', 'It needs no water or cooking, so it does not become contaminated.', 2),
(45, 'Outpatient', 'What are the cure criteria for SAM?', 'A MUAC of 12.5 cm or more and/or a weight-for-height of −2 standard deviations or more (the same measure as at admission), with no oedema, on 2 consecutive visits.', 2),
(46, 'Outpatient', 'How is moderate acute malnutrition treated?', 'In a targeted supplementary feeding programme, with ready-to-use supplementary food (RUSF) or corn-soya blend, and visits every 2 weeks.', 2),
(47, 'Outpatient', 'What is the first sign of recovery in SAM?', 'The return of appetite and smiling, at days 3–4.', 2),
],
'rickets': [
(0, 'Definition', 'What is rickets?', 'Defective mineralisation of growing bone (the growth plate and the osteoid) in children.', 1),
(1, 'Definition', 'What is the same defect called after the growth plates have closed?', 'Osteomalacia.', 1),
(2, 'Vitamin D', 'Where is vitamin D 25-hydroxylated?', 'In the liver.', 1),
(3, 'Vitamin D', 'Where is 25-hydroxyvitamin D converted to the active form?', 'In the kidney, by 1α-hydroxylase, which is stimulated by parathyroid hormone (PTH).', 1),
(4, 'Vitamin D', 'What is the best test of vitamin D status?', 'Serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D.', 1),
(5, 'Vitamin D', 'Which 25-hydroxyvitamin D level defines vitamin D deficiency?', 'A level below 30 nmol/L.', 1),
(6, 'Causes', 'What is the most common cause of rickets?', 'Nutritional rickets: vitamin D deficiency and/or a low calcium intake.', 1),
(7, 'Causes', 'What is the peak age of nutritional rickets?', 'From 6 months to 2 years.', 1),
(8, 'Causes', 'Why does an exclusively breastfed infant develop rickets without supplements?', 'Breast milk is low in vitamin D.', 2),
(9, 'Causes', 'How do anticonvulsants cause rickets?', 'Phenytoin and phenobarbitone speed up the breakdown of vitamin D.', 2),
(10, 'Causes', 'What is the defect in vitamin D-dependent rickets type 1?', 'Absent renal 1α-hydroxylase, so the level of 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D is low.', 3),
(11, 'Causes', 'What is the clue to vitamin D-dependent rickets type 2?', 'Alopecia with a very high 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D level, because of resistance of the vitamin D receptor.', 3),
(12, 'Causes', 'What is the commonest inherited form of rickets?', 'X-linked hypophosphataemic rickets: the kidney loses phosphate and the PTH is normal.', 2),
(13, 'Clinical', 'What is the earliest clinical sign of rickets in a young infant?', 'Craniotabes: a soft skull that yields like a ping-pong ball.', 1),
(14, 'Clinical', 'What is the rachitic rosary?', 'Rounded, painless swelling of the costochondral junctions.', 1),
(15, 'Clinical', 'What is the Harrison sulcus?', 'A groove of the lower chest along the attachment of the diaphragm.', 1),
(16, 'Clinical', 'What are the fontanelle and head findings in rickets?', 'A wide, late-closing anterior fontanelle, with frontal and parietal bossing.', 1),
(17, 'Clinical', 'Which leg deformity appears after the child walks?', 'Bow legs (genu varum), and later knock knees.', 1),
(18, 'Clinical', 'What are the muscle and motor signs of rickets?', 'Hypotonia, delayed motor milestones and a pot belly.', 2),
(19, 'Clinical', 'How does the rosary of scurvy differ from the rosary of rickets?', 'In scurvy it is sharp, angular and tender; in rickets it is rounded and painless.', 3),
(20, 'Investigations', 'What is the biochemistry of nutritional rickets?', 'Calcium normal or low, phosphate low, alkaline phosphatase (ALP) and PTH high, and 25-hydroxyvitamin D low.', 2),
(21, 'Investigations', 'What is the best blood marker of rickets activity and healing?', 'Alkaline phosphatase (ALP).', 1),
(22, 'Investigations', 'Which finding is NOT consistent with nutritional rickets?', 'A raised serum phosphate, which is seen in chronic kidney disease.', 3),
(23, 'Investigations', 'Why is the serum calcium often normal in rickets?', 'The raised PTH mobilises calcium from bone (and makes the kidney lose phosphate).', 3),
(24, 'Investigations', 'What are the X-ray signs of active rickets at the wrist?', 'A widened, cupped and frayed metaphysis with low bone density.', 2),
(25, 'Investigations', 'What is the first X-ray sign of healing?', 'A dense white line of calcification at the metaphysis, 2–4 weeks after vitamin D is started.', 2),
(26, 'Investigations', 'What is the biochemistry of X-linked hypophosphataemic rickets?', 'A very low phosphate, with normal calcium and normal PTH.', 3),
(27, 'Treatment', 'What daily vitamin D treatment is given to a 2-year-old with rickets?', '3000–6000 IU daily for 12 weeks, together with calcium 500 mg daily.', 2),
(28, 'Treatment', 'What daily vitamin D treatment is given under 12 months of age?', '2000 IU daily for 12 weeks, together with calcium.', 2),
(29, 'Treatment', 'What is the single oral (stoss) dose of vitamin D for children aged 1–12 years?', '150 000 IU once (50 000 IU at 3–12 months).', 2),
(30, 'Treatment', 'When is stoss therapy used?', 'When daily drops are unlikely to be taken. It is not used under 3 months of age.', 2),
(31, 'Treatment', 'What calcium dose is given with vitamin D treatment?', '500 mg of elemental calcium daily.', 1),
(32, 'Treatment', 'How is hypocalcaemic tetany or a hypocalcaemic seizure treated in an emergency?', '10% calcium gluconate 0.5 ml/kg intravenously (maximum 20 ml) over 10 minutes, with cardiac monitoring.', 2),
(33, 'Treatment', 'What is the drug of choice in vitamin D-dependent rickets?', 'Calcitriol, together with calcium.', 2),
(34, 'Treatment', 'How is X-linked hypophosphataemic rickets treated?', 'Oral phosphate with calcitriol, or burosumab.', 2),
(35, 'Treatment', 'When is healing checked, and how?', 'At 3 months, with the ALP and a wrist X-ray.', 2),
(36, 'Prevention', 'How much vitamin D is given to prevent rickets in infancy?', '400 IU daily from birth to 12 months, however the baby is fed.', 1),
(37, 'Prevention', 'What are the features of vitamin D toxicity?', 'Hypercalcaemia: vomiting, constipation, polyuria and nephrocalcinosis.', 2),
(38, 'Physiology', 'What is the main action of active vitamin D on the gut?', 'It increases the absorption of calcium and phosphate from the small intestine.', 1),
(39, 'Physiology', 'What are the effects of PTH on the kidney?', 'It increases calcium reabsorption, decreases phosphate reabsorption (phosphaturia) and increases 1α-hydroxylase activity.', 2),
(40, 'Biochemistry', 'Why is the phosphate low in nutritional rickets?', 'The low calcium raises PTH, which makes the kidney lose phosphate.', 3),
],
'micronutrients': [
(0, 'Vitamin A', 'What is the earliest symptom of vitamin A deficiency?', 'Night blindness.', 1),
(1, 'Vitamin A', 'What are Bitot spots?', 'Foamy grey-white patches on the temporal bulbar conjunctiva (stage X1B).', 1),
(2, 'Vitamin A', 'What is the most severe eye stage of vitamin A deficiency?', 'Keratomalacia: ulceration and melting of the cornea, leading to blindness.', 1),
(3, 'Vitamin A', 'What is the skin sign of vitamin A deficiency?', 'Dry, rough skin (follicular hyperkeratosis).', 1),
(4, 'Vitamin A', 'How is xerophthalmia treated?', 'The vitamin A dose for age, by mouth, on day 1, day 2 and day 14.', 2),
(5, 'Vitamin A', 'What is the vitamin A treatment dose for a child aged 12 months or older?', '200 000 IU (100 000 IU at 6–11 months; 50 000 IU under 6 months).', 1),
(6, 'Vitamin A', 'How is vitamin A given in measles?', 'Every child with measles receives the dose for age on day 1 and day 2.', 2),
(7, 'Vitamin A', 'What is the vitamin A supplementation schedule (WHO, EPI)?', '100 000 IU at 6–11 months, then 200 000 IU every 6 months until 5 years of age.', 1),
(8, 'Vitamin A', 'What are the features of acute vitamin A toxicity?', 'Raised intracranial pressure: a bulging fontanelle and vomiting.', 2),
(9, 'Vitamin A', 'What are the features of chronic vitamin A toxicity?', 'Hair loss, dry itchy skin, bone pain and a large liver. Vitamin A is teratogenic in pregnancy.', 2),
(10, 'Zinc', 'What is the zinc dose in diarrhoea?', 'Under 6 months: 10 mg daily. From 6 months: 20 mg daily. It is given for 10–14 days.', 1),
(11, 'Zinc', 'What is the benefit of zinc in diarrhoea?', 'It shortens the episode and prevents new episodes for 2–3 months.', 2),
(12, 'Zinc', 'How is acrodermatitis enteropathica inherited?', 'It is an autosomal recessive defect of zinc absorption.', 2),
(13, 'Zinc', 'What is the triad of acrodermatitis enteropathica?', 'A rash around the mouth, anus, hands and feet; diarrhoea; and hair loss.', 2),
(14, 'Zinc', 'When does acrodermatitis enteropathica start?', 'At weaning from breast milk.', 3),
(15, 'Zinc', 'How is acrodermatitis enteropathica treated?', 'With oral zinc for life.', 2),
(16, 'Zinc', 'Which enzyme is zinc-dependent and may stay low in malnutrition?', 'Alkaline phosphatase.', 3),
(17, 'Iodine', 'What is the commonest preventable cause of intellectual disability in the world?', 'Iodine deficiency.', 1),
(18, 'Iodine', 'What are the features of endemic cretinism?', 'Deaf-mutism, intellectual disability, short stature and spasticity.', 2),
(19, 'Iodine', 'What are the effects of iodine deficiency in children?', 'Goitre, hypothyroidism and poor school performance.', 1),
(20, 'Iodine', 'How is iodine deficiency prevented?', 'With iodised salt in every household.', 1),
(21, 'Minerals', 'What is the commonest micronutrient deficiency?', 'Iron deficiency.', 1),
(22, 'Minerals', 'What does selenium deficiency cause?', 'A cardiomyopathy (Keshan disease).', 2),
(23, 'Minerals', 'What are the effects of too little and too much fluoride?', 'Too little causes dental caries; too much causes mottled teeth (fluorosis).', 1),
(24, 'Vitamins B', 'What does thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency cause?', 'Beriberi: wet beriberi (heart failure and oedema) or dry beriberi (neuropathy).', 2),
(25, 'Vitamins B', 'What does riboflavin (vitamin B2) deficiency cause?', 'Angular stomatitis, cheilosis, glossitis and vascularisation of the cornea.', 2),
(26, 'Vitamins B', 'What are the features of pellagra, and which vitamin is deficient?', 'Dermatitis of sun-exposed skin, diarrhoea and dementia; it is niacin (vitamin B3) deficiency.', 2),
(27, 'Vitamins B', 'What does pyridoxine (vitamin B6) deficiency cause?', 'Seizures in infants, peripheral neuropathy during isoniazid treatment and microcytic anaemia.', 2),
(28, 'Vitamins B', 'A newborn has seizures that do not respond to anticonvulsants. What should be tried?', 'Pyridoxine 100 mg intravenously.', 3),
(31, 'Vitamin C', 'What are the features of scurvy in an infant?', 'Painful legs with pseudoparalysis, bleeding gums and a tender rosary.', 2),
(32, 'Vitamin C', 'How is scurvy treated?', 'Vitamin C 100–200 mg daily, with citrus fruits.', 1),
(33, 'Vitamin C', 'When are bleeding gums seen in scurvy?', 'Only after the teeth have erupted.', 3),
(34, 'Fat-soluble', 'What does vitamin E deficiency cause in preterm infants?', 'Haemolytic anaemia.', 2),
(35, 'Fat-soluble', 'Which clotting factors depend on vitamin K?', 'Factors II, VII, IX and X.', 1),
(36, 'Fat-soluble', 'How is vitamin K deficiency bleeding prevented?', 'Vitamin K 1 mg intramuscularly at birth.', 1),
(37, 'Fat-soluble', 'Which vitamins can be toxic, and why?', 'The fat-soluble vitamins (A and D), because they are stored in the body.', 2),
],
}

